---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import AuthorsCitation from '../../components/AuthorsCitation.astro';
import '../../styles/section.css';
import '../../styles/hero.css';
import '../../styles/authors.css';
import '../../styles/resources.css';
import '../../styles/lagen.css';

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const asset = (name: string) => `${basePath}lagen/${name}`;
const pageTitle = 'LAGEN: Latency-Aware Agents';
const citation = `@misc{latencyawareagents2026,
  title={The Cost of Thinking: Evaluating Latency Sensitivity in Real-Time Foundation Model Agents},
  author={Anonymous Authors},
  year={2026},
  note={Anonymous research draft}
}`;
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description="LAGEN: Latency-Aware Agents" project="lagen">
  <section id="hero" class="content-section hero-section">
    <div class="title-wrapper">
      <div class="title-with-logo">
        <img src={asset('favicon.svg')} alt="LAGEN Logo" class="title-logo" />
        <h1 class="main-title" id="animated-title">LAGEN</h1>
      </div>
      <p class="title-subtitle">Latency-Aware Agents</p>
    </div>

    <p class="main-subtitle">
      <strong>The Cost of Thinking</strong>: evaluating latency sensitivity in real-time foundation model agents.
    </p>
    <p class="main-subtitle lagen-summary">
      A correct action can still fail when inference moves it onto the wrong state. We profile the deployment clock,
      replay its temporal structure, and condition agents on the latency regime they must control through.
    </p>

    <div class="authors-section compact">
      <div class="authors-list">
        <p class="author-line">
          <a href="https://zihanwang314.github.io/">Zenus Wang</a><sup>*&dagger;1</sup>,
          <a href="https://chigui0.github.io/">Chi Gui</a><sup>&dagger;2</sup>,
          <a href="#">Xing Jin</a><sup>&dagger;3</sup>,
          <a href="https://qinengwang-aiden.github.io/">Qineng Wang</a><sup>&dagger;1</sup>,
          <a href="https://lichengliu03.github.io/">Licheng Liu</a><sup>4</sup>,
          <a href="https://jameskrw.github.io/">Kangrui Wang</a><sup>1</sup>
        </p>
        <p class="author-line">
          <a href="https://shiqichen17.github.io/">Shiqi Chen</a><sup>5</sup>,
          <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=WR875gYAAAAJ&hl=en">Linjie Li</a><sup>6</sup>,
          <a href="https://zyang-ur.github.io/">Zhengyuan Yang</a><sup>7</sup>,
          <a href="https://williamzhangsjtu.github.io/">Pingyue Zhang</a><sup>1</sup>,
          <a href="https://2prime.github.io/">Yiping Lu</a><sup>1</sup>,
          <a href="https://jiajunwu.com/">Jiajun Wu</a><sup>8</sup>,
          <a href="https://profiles.stanford.edu/fei-fei-li">Li Fei-Fei</a><sup>8</sup>
        </p>
        <p class="author-line">
          <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/people/lijuanw/">Lijuan Wang</a><sup>7</sup>,
          <a href="https://homes.cs.washington.edu/~yejin/">Yejin Choi</a><sup>8</sup>,
          <a href="https://limanling.github.io/">Manling Li</a><sup>1</sup>
        </p>
        <p class="equal-contribution"><sup>&dagger;</sup> Core Contributors &nbsp;&nbsp; <sup>*</sup> Project Lead</p>
        <p class="affiliations"><sup>1</sup>Northwestern University &nbsp; <sup>2</sup>UIUC &nbsp; <sup>3</sup>University of British Columbia &nbsp; <sup>4</sup>Imperial College London &nbsp; <sup>5</sup>City University of Hong Kong &nbsp; <sup>6</sup>University of Washington &nbsp; <sup>7</sup>Microsoft &nbsp; <sup>8</sup>Stanford University</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="resources-section">
      <div class="resources-buttons">
        <a href={asset('Latency_Aware_Agents_Paper_Draft.pdf')} class="resource-button" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          <i class="fas fa-file-alt resource-icon" aria-hidden="true"></i><span>Paper</span>
        </a>
        <a href="#citation" class="resource-button">
          <i class="fas fa-clipboard resource-icon" aria-hidden="true"></i><span>Citation</span>
        </a>
        <a href="https://www.mll.lab.northwestern.edu/" class="resource-button" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          <i class="fas fa-external-link-alt resource-icon" aria-hidden="true"></i><span>MLL Lab</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="teaser-figure">
      <video class="teaser-image teaser-video" src={asset('demos/causal-fork.mp4')} poster={asset('demos/hero-teaser.webp')} muted autoplay loop playsinline preload="metadata" aria-label="The same Flappy Bird scene under two action-execution clocks"></video>
      <figcaption class="lagen-figcaption"><span>Same observation, different execution clocks</span><a href="#results">Inspect the evidence &darr;</a></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="method" class="content-section alt-background lagen-section">
    <div class="section-container">
      <h2 class="section-title">Method</h2>
      <div class="method-block">
        <div class="method-block-header"><span class="method-tag">Protocol</span><h3>Separate the observation clock from the inference clock</h3></div>
        <div class="method-block-body">
          <p class="method-desc">We decouple environment cadence <em>T<sub>k</sub></em> from inference delay <em>&delta;<sub>k</sub></em>, so fixed delays, stochastic jitter, and measured hardware traces become reproducible evaluation conditions.</p>
          <img src={asset('figures/protocol.png')} alt="Observation cadence and inference latency in a real-time agent" class="paper-fig" loading="lazy" />
          <p class="fig-caption"><strong>Protocol.</strong> A decision is executed when it arrives, not when it is requested. World-pausing evaluation is the <em>&delta; = 0</em> special case.</p>
          <div class="equation-box lagen-equation"><span><strong>T<sub>k</sub></strong> observation cadence</span><span><strong>&delta;<sub>k</sub></strong> inference delay</span><span><strong>&tau;<sub>k</sub></strong> action arrival time</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="method-block">
        <div class="method-block-header"><span class="method-tag">Deployment</span><h3>Profile once, replay the temporal structure</h3></div>
        <div class="method-block-body"><p class="method-desc">End-to-end latency is treated as part of the task condition. A measured profile can be replayed on a faster host for controlled policy comparisons and same-device calibration.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="results" class="content-section lagen-section">
    <div class="section-container">
      <h2 class="section-title">Experiments</h2>
      <p class="section-subtitle">Six questions about how deployment timing changes agent behavior.</p>

      <div class="rq-block" id="rq1">
        <button class="rq-header" type="button" aria-expanded="false" data-target="rq1-body"><span class="rq-badge">RQ1</span><h3>Does inference latency change policy performance?</h3><span class="rq-summary">A small train-evaluation mismatch can erase a policy's advantage.</span><span class="rq-chevron" aria-hidden="true">&#8964;</span></button>
        <div class="rq-body" id="rq1-body"><h4 class="rq-subheading">Performance collapses under delayed action arrival</h4><img src={asset('figures/vla-degradation.png')} alt="VLA performance under increasing evaluation latency" class="rq-fig" loading="lazy" /><p class="rq-caption">The same visual policy can move from a strong zero-latency score to near-zero return after a small execution delay.</p><img src={asset('figures/flappy-heatmap.png')} alt="Train-evaluation latency heatmaps for Flappy Bird" class="rq-fig" loading="lazy" /><p class="rq-caption">Performance concentrates near matched latency conditions rather than forming a latency-invariant policy.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="rq-block" id="rq2">
        <button class="rq-header" type="button" aria-expanded="false" data-target="rq2-body"><span class="rq-badge">RQ2</span><h3>Why is jitter worse than a fixed delay with the same mean?</h3><span class="rq-summary">Temporal tails matter when a late action misses the control window.</span><span class="rq-chevron" aria-hidden="true">&#8964;</span></button>
        <div class="rq-body" id="rq2-body"><h4 class="rq-subheading">Same mean, different fate</h4><img src={asset('figures/jitter-destructiveness.png')} alt="Policy performance under fixed and stochastic latency profiles" class="rq-fig" loading="lazy" /><p class="rq-caption">Holding mean delay fixed, stochastic profiles reduce return beyond a fixed delay. Report the profile, not only mean inference time.</p><video src={asset('demos/jitter-replay.mp4')} controls muted playsinline preload="metadata" class="rq-video" aria-label="Fixed and sampled latency replay"></video></div>
      </div>

      <div class="rq-block" id="rq3">
        <button class="rq-header" type="button" aria-expanded="false" data-target="rq3-body"><span class="rq-badge">RQ3</span><h3>Can broader latency exposure improve robustness?</h3><span class="rq-summary">Mixed-latency training lifts delayed evaluation, but task sensitivity sets the ceiling.</span><span class="rq-chevron" aria-hidden="true">&#8964;</span></button>
        <div class="rq-body" id="rq3-body"><img src={asset('figures/multilatency-training.png')} alt="Train-evaluation latency matrices across three environments" class="rq-fig" loading="lazy" /><p class="rq-caption">Robustness is task-dependent: broader exposure flattens degradation without always restoring the zero-latency peak.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="rq-block" id="rq4">
        <button class="rq-header" type="button" aria-expanded="false" data-target="rq4-body"><span class="rq-badge">RQ4</span><h3>Does explicit latency conditioning help?</h3><span class="rq-summary">A timing channel helps where the task leaves room to adapt.</span><span class="rq-chevron" aria-hidden="true">&#8964;</span></button>
        <div class="rq-body" id="rq4-body"><img src={asset('figures/latency-conditioning.png')} alt="OpenVLA performance with and without explicit latency information" class="rq-fig" loading="lazy" /><p class="rq-caption">Explicit latency information gives the policy a chance to adapt its control to the deployment regime.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="rq-block" id="rq5">
        <button class="rq-header" type="button" aria-expanded="false" data-target="rq5-body"><span class="rq-badge">RQ5</span><h3>Can temporal context compensate for stale observations?</h3><span class="rq-summary">More history is not automatically better; useful temporal resolution is task-dependent.</span><span class="rq-chevron" aria-hidden="true">&#8964;</span></button>
        <div class="rq-body" id="rq5-body"><img src={asset('figures/temporal-memory.png')} alt="Temporal memory input comparisons and final post-train reward" class="rq-fig" loading="lazy" /><p class="rq-caption">KV memory is competitive in this study, while frame stacks and stitched inputs are not uniformly useful.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="rq-block" id="rq6">
        <button class="rq-header" type="button" aria-expanded="false" data-target="rq6-body"><span class="rq-badge">RQ6</span><h3>Can measured hardware profiles be replayed?</h3><span class="rq-summary">Hardware changes the shape and scale of end-to-end delay.</span><span class="rq-chevron" aria-hidden="true">&#8964;</span></button>
        <div class="rq-body" id="rq6-body"><img src={asset('figures/profile-calibration.png')} alt="Latency profiles across hardware and same-device calibration results" class="rq-fig" loading="lazy" /><p class="rq-caption">Distinct hardware produces distinct latency distributions; replay preserves the deployment condition for controlled comparisons.</p><img src={asset('figures/profile-replay-status.png')} alt="Cross-device replay draft status" class="rq-fig" loading="lazy" /><p class="rq-caption"><strong>Draft status.</strong> Same-device calibration is populated. Entries marked “sim-cross pending” are not completed cross-device results.</p><div class="evidence-grid"><div class="evidence-item"><span class="evidence-num">!</span><span>Cross-device prediction remains an open experiment in the supplied draft artifact.</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section alt-background lagen-section">
    <div class="section-container"><h2 class="section-title">Conclusion</h2><div class="lagen-takeaways"><div><strong>01</strong><p>Latency changes which state receives an otherwise valid action.</p></div><div><strong>02</strong><p>Mean delay alone cannot reproduce destructive temporal tails.</p></div><div><strong>03</strong><p>Timing-aware training and explicit conditioning make deployment latency controllable.</p></div></div></div>
  </section>

  <AuthorsCitation citation={citation} />
</Layout>

<script>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.rq-header[data-target]').forEach((header) => {
      header.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const targetId = header.dataset.target!;
        const body = document.getElementById(targetId)!;
        const open = header.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';
        document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.rq-header[data-target]').forEach((other) => {
          const otherBody = document.getElementById(other.dataset.target!);
          other.setAttribute('aria-expanded', other === header && !open ? 'true' : 'false');
          if (otherBody) otherBody.style.maxHeight = other === header && !open ? `${otherBody.scrollHeight}px` : '0px';
        });
      });
    });
  });
</script>
